<div class="bb-quote"><b>JesterOC wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class="bb-quote-body">I hope this Kickstarter does not take as long as the Oblivaeon box set. Have they stated that the book text is complete and that the Kickstarter is only there to allow for a large upfront print run?
&nbsp;
</blockquote></div>
In general, Kickstarters for RPGs tend to deliver much faster than those for board games, simply because there are so few physical components.&nbsp; While the art and layout can be time consuming, many of the notable rpg companies--and it sounds like &gt; G and Critical Hits are doing this as well--often prep the pdf prior to the KS launch so that, as soon as the drive ends, they can send backers PDF copies of the books.I imagine we&#39;ll see digital versions of the rules--at least in beta form--during or shortly after the KS ends.

[/quote]

Added piece making this possibly go faster is that the printing of the books would be US based instead of being done in China. &nbsp; Once the book is done that saves at least a month or more in travel time for them to get it at their warehouse. &nbsp;

[quote="Arcanist Lupus"]

Also, this Kickstarter is unlikely to double in size in the stretch goals the way Oblivaeon did.

[/quote]

{VaderVoice}:I find your lack of faith... disturbing.&nbsp;

But seriously, RPG kickstarters tend to set a pretty low base goal for the books themselves, and go through loads of stretch goals. (Check the Savage Rifts, Torg Eternity or Delta Green kickstarters for examples.)&nbsp;

However, unlike the card game where everything is expected to be in one box, RPGs tend to add other books (or things like fancy character sheets, sample characters, adventures, etc) to the line. The core book itself may get bigger due to KS stretch goals, of course, but it&#39;ll be stuff that builds on what they&#39;ve already developed. So more villains or environments, maybe more NPC heroes, maybe info on pre-Oblivaeon stuff. But that&#39;s just expanding the toy box, not inventing new toys.&nbsp;

Also, RPG kickstarters tend to give backers something, even if it&#39;s a plain text beta document, as soon as the check from Kickstarter clears.&nbsp;

